      "rationale": "Applicability remains provisionally supported by the explicit producer-consumer inference. Recovery assigns commands to the instance and publishes them for acceptance at the same execution position; matching replies can commit those commands without a comparison against the earlier decision, and ordered execution distinguishes PUT from NONE. The README supplies orientation rather than an internal assignment contract. The selected constructor enables execution, recovery dispatch has no leadership guard, and N=3 with F=1 yields thresholds of two. The newly attached transport provides no mandatory synchronization requiring protective leader traffic to arrive before recovery between the nonleaders. A concrete source-derived candidate is to pause replica 0's run goroutine after its supported COMMITTED transition and before bcastCommit enters SendMsg. This avoids deliberately holding its send mutex. With successful finite writes to the connected leader, replica 1 can complete its preparation broadcast while replica 0's listener receives independently and replica 2 supplies a matching PrepareReply. This is a candidate schedule, not established reachability. It makes the protective branches insufficient, on source evidence alone, to dismiss the inferred publication-boundary responsibility.",
      "counterevidence": [
        "Assignment preservation is stronger than final agreement if another mechanism reliably suppresses conflicting proposals. The attached sources do not establish such suppression for this candidate.",
        "A committed participant responds to Prepare with a full Commit; handleAccept preserves committed instances and rejects lower ballots. These paths remain capable of preventing particular continuations.",
        "The overwrite comment acknowledges behavior without specifying an alternative guarantee.",
        "Normal bcastCommit sends Commit.Marshal bytes under commitShortRPC. For the scoped singleton PUT, CommitShort reads the original ballot as Count and bytes 02 01 00 00 as ballot 258. Remaining bytes stay in the stream. This can obstruct recovery or decoding if delivered; it cannot be replaced with intended short-commit behavior.",
        "SendMsg ignores write and Flush errors. Returning from it does not establish successful transmission."
      ],
